window.PHYSICS_BANK=[{"q":"A force of 8 N acts on a mass of 3 kg. What is its acceleration? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"2.67","unit":"m/s²","d1":"11","d2":"24","d3":"3","explain":"Rearrange F = ma to a = F/m, so a = 8 / 3 = 2.67 m/s².","formula":"F = ma","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Adding force and mass instead of dividing, or multiplying them."},{"q":"What is the weight of a 5 kg mass on Earth? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"49","unit":"N","d1":"14.8","d2":"0.51","d3":"5","explain":"Weight is W = mg, so W = 5 x 9.8 = 49 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Giving the mass in kilograms, or adding g instead of multiplying."},{"q":"A box is pushed with 12 N to the right and 5 N to the left. What is the resultant force?","a":"7 N","unit":"N, to the right","d1":"17 N right","d2":"60 N right","d3":"7 N left","explain":"Forces in opposite directions subtract, so 12 - 5 = 7 N to the right.","formula":"F_net = F1 - F2","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Adding the forces because both are given, or reversing the direction."},{"q":"Two forces of 8 N and 6 N act on an object in the same direction. What is the resultant force?","a":"14","unit":"N","d1":"2","d2":"48","d3":"7","explain":"Forces in the same direction add, so 8 + 6 = 14 N.","formula":"F_net = F1 + F2","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Subtracting because the numbers differ, or multiplying them."},{"q":"A force of 40 N acts 0.3 m from a pivot. What is the moment?","a":"12","unit":"N m","d1":"133.33","d2":"120","d3":"40.3","explain":"Moment = force x perpendicular distance, so 40 x 0.3 = 12 N m.","formula":"M = Fd","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing force by distance, or using centimetres as metres."},{"q":"What is the weight of a 2 kg bag on Earth? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"19.6","unit":"N","d1":"2","d2":"9.8","d3":"0.2","explain":"Weight is W = mg, so W = 2 x 9.8 = 19.6 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Giving the mass, or giving g alone."},{"q":"A spring is 20 cm long with no load and 35 cm long with a load. What is its extension?","a":"15","unit":"cm","d1":"55","d2":"35","d3":"20","explain":"Extension = stretched length - original length = 35 - 20 = 15 cm.","formula":"x = L - L0","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Adding the lengths, or giving the total stretched length."},{"q":"A rope pulls up with 30 N while gravity pulls down with 30 N. What is the resultant force?","a":"0","unit":"N","d1":"60","d2":"30","d3":"900","explain":"Equal and opposite forces cancel, so the resultant is 0 N.","formula":"F_net = F_up - F_down","topic":"forces","tier":1,"trap":"Adding forces that point in opposite directions."},{"q":"A resultant force of 30 N acts on a 6 kg mass. What is the acceleration?","a":"5","unit":"m/s²","d1":"180","d2":"0.2","d3":"24","explain":"a = F/m = 30 / 6 = 5 m/s².","formula":"F = ma","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying instead of dividing, or dividing in the wrong order."},{"q":"An object weighs 98 N on Earth. What is its mass? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"10","unit":"kg","d1":"960.4","d2":"88.2","d3":"98","explain":"Rearrange W = mg to m = W/g, so m = 98 / 9.8 = 10 kg.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying by g, or reporting the weight as the mass."},{"q":"A force of 24 N stretches a spring by 6 cm. What is the spring constant in N/m?","a":"400","unit":"N/m","d1":"4","d2":"1.44","d3":"0.0025","explain":"Convert 6 cm to 0.06 m, then k = F/x = 24 / 0.06 = 400 N/m.","formula":"F = kx","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Leaving the extension in centimetres, which gives N/cm."},{"q":"A spring of constant 150 N/m is stretched by a force of 12 N. What is its extension?","a":"0.08","unit":"m","d1":"1800","d2":"12.5","d3":"1.25","explain":"x = F/k = 12 / 150 = 0.08 m.","formula":"F = kx","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying force by constant, or inverting the formula."},{"q":"A 60 N weight sits 0.2 m from a pivot. What force at 0.6 m on the other side balances it?","a":"20","unit":"N","d1":"180","d2":"12","d3":"30","explain":"Balance moments: F1d1 = F2d2, so F2 = (60 x 0.2) / 0.6 = 20 N.","formula":"F1d1 = F2d2","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the distance ratio, or reporting the moment instead of the force."},{"q":"Two perpendicular forces of 3 N and 4 N act on a point. What is the resultant?","a":"5","unit":"N","d1":"7","d2":"1","d3":"12","explain":"Perpendicular forces add as a vector triangle: R = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5 N.","formula":"R = sqrt(F1^2 + F2^2)","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Adding the magnitudes directly, or subtracting them."},{"q":"A 60 kg astronaut is on the Moon, where g = 1.6 m/s². What is her weight there?","a":"96","unit":"N","d1":"588","d2":"60","d3":"37.5","explain":"Weight depends on local gravity: W = mg = 60 x 1.6 = 96 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Using Earth's g, or giving the mass which does not change."},{"q":"A crane lifts a 500 N load at constant speed. What is the tension in the cable?","a":"500","unit":"N","d1":"0","d2":"1000","d3":"250","explain":"At constant velocity the forces balance, so tension equals weight, 500 N.","formula":"F_net = 0 at constant velocity","topic":"forces","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming a moving object has no forces, or doubling the weight."},{"q":"A force of 8 N acts on a 250 g mass. What is the acceleration?","a":"32","unit":"m/s²","d1":"2000","d2":"2","d3":"0.0312","explain":"Convert 250 g to 0.25 kg, then a = F/m = 8 / 0.25 = 32 m/s².","formula":"F = ma","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting to convert grams to kilograms."},{"q":"A 50 kg trolley is pushed with 500 N while friction is 100 N. What is its acceleration?","a":"8","unit":"m/s²","d1":"10","d2":"2","d3":"400","explain":"Find the resultant first: 500 - 100 = 400 N, then a = 400 / 50 = 8 m/s².","formula":"F_net = ma","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Using the applied force without subtracting friction."},{"q":"A spring of constant 250 N/m is stretched by 12 cm. What force is needed?","a":"30","unit":"N","d1":"3000","d2":"2080","d3":"0.0005","explain":"Convert 12 cm to 0.12 m, then F = kx = 250 x 0.12 = 30 N.","formula":"F = kx","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Using centimetres directly, which gives 3000 N."},{"q":"Two perpendicular forces of 5 N and 12 N act on a point. What is the resultant?","a":"13","unit":"N","d1":"17","d2":"7","d3":"60","explain":"R = sqrt(5^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(169) = 13 N.","formula":"R = sqrt(F1^2 + F2^2)","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Adding the magnitudes without using Pythagoras."},{"q":"A 25 N force acts 40 cm from a pivot. What is the moment in N m?","a":"10","unit":"N m","d1":"1000","d2":"62.5","d3":"65","explain":"Convert 40 cm to 0.40 m, then M = Fd = 25 x 0.40 = 10 N m.","formula":"M = Fd","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Using centimetres, which gives 1000 N cm rather than N m."},{"q":"A car of mass 1500 kg is lifted. What is its weight in kN? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"14.7","unit":"kN","d1":"1500","d2":"14700","d3":"147","explain":"W = mg = 1500 x 9.8 = 14700 N, and 1 kN = 1000 N, so 14.7 kN.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Not converting newtons to kilonewtons, or dividing by 100."},{"q":"A 40 kg child sits 1.5 m from a pivot. How far from the pivot must a 60 kg child sit to balance?","a":"1","unit":"m","d1":"3600","d2":"2.25","d3":"1.5","explain":"The g cancels in m1d1 = m2d2, so d2 = (40 x 1.5) / 60 = 1.0 m.","formula":"m1d1 = m2d2","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting that g cancels, or inverting the mass ratio."},{"q":"A 2 kg book rests on a table. What is the normal reaction force? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"19.6","unit":"N","d1":"2","d2":"9.8","d3":"0","explain":"The book is at rest, so normal reaction balances weight: N = mg = 19.6 N.","formula":"N = mg","topic":"forces","tier":3,"trap":"Saying the reaction is zero because the book does not move."},{"q":"A runner travels 150 m in 25 s. What is the average speed?","a":"6","unit":"m/s","d1":"3750","d2":"0.167","d3":"175","explain":"Average speed = distance / time = 150 / 25 = 6 m/s.","formula":"v = d/t","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying distance by time, or dividing time by distance."},{"q":"A cyclist rides at 12 m/s for 5 s. How far does she travel?","a":"60","unit":"m","d1":"2.4","d2":"17","d3":"120","explain":"Distance = speed x time = 12 x 5 = 60 m.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing speed by time instead of multiplying."},{"q":"A car travels 100 m at 4 m/s. How long does it take?","a":"25","unit":"s","d1":"400","d2":"0.04","d3":"104","explain":"Time = distance / speed = 100 / 4 = 25 s.","formula":"t = d/v","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying distance by speed, or adding them."},{"q":"A car speeds up from 5 m/s to 20 m/s in 4 s. What is its acceleration?","a":"3.75","unit":"m/s²","d1":"6.25","d2":"5","d3":"80","explain":"a = (v - u) / t = (20 - 5) / 4 = 3.75 m/s².","formula":"a = (v - u)/t","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Adding the speeds, or using only the final speed."},{"q":"A train slows from 30 m/s to 10 m/s in 5 s. What is its deceleration?","a":"4","unit":"m/s²","d1":"8","d2":"6","d3":"20","explain":"Change in velocity is 30 - 10 = 20 m/s over 5 s, so a = 4 m/s².","formula":"a = (v - u)/t","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Adding the speeds, or failing to divide by the time."},{"q":"On a speed-time graph a vehicle moves at a steady 8 m/s for 6 s. How far does it go?","a":"48","unit":"m","d1":"14","d2":"1.33","d3":"24","explain":"The area under the graph is a rectangle: 8 x 6 = 48 m.","formula":"distance = area under graph","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Using the triangle formula on a rectangle."},{"q":"A coach covers 240 km in 3 hours. What is its average speed?","a":"80","unit":"km/h","d1":"720","d2":"0.0125","d3":"237","explain":"Average speed = 240 / 3 = 80 km/h.","formula":"v = d/t","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying instead of dividing."},{"q":"A car travels at 72 km/h. What is this in m/s?","a":"20","unit":"m/s","d1":"72","d2":"259.2","d3":"7.2","explain":"Divide by 3.6: 72 / 3.6 = 20 m/s.","formula":"km/h to m/s: divide by 3.6","topic":"motion","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying by 3.6, or only moving the decimal point."},{"q":"A car starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 7 s. What is its final speed?","a":"21","unit":"m/s","d1":"10","d2":"42","d3":"0.429","explain":"v = u + at with u = 0, so v = 3 x 7 = 21 m/s.","formula":"v = u + at","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Adding acceleration and time, or forgetting u = 0."},{"q":"A cyclist starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² over 25 m. What is the final speed?","a":"10","unit":"m/s","d1":"100","d2":"50","d3":"7.07","explain":"Use v² = u² + 2as with u = 0: v² = 100, so v = 10 m/s.","formula":"v² = u² + 2as","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Reporting v² as the speed, or taking the square root of as alone."},{"q":"An object starts from rest and accelerates at 4 m/s² for 3 s. How far does it travel?","a":"18","unit":"m","d1":"36","d2":"12","d3":"6","explain":"s = ut + ½at² with u = 0, so s = 0.5 x 4 x 9 = 18 m.","formula":"s = ut + ½at²","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Forgetting the ½, which doubles the distance."},{"q":"An object has initial speed 5 m/s and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 4 s. How far does it travel?","a":"36","unit":"m","d1":"20","d2":"16","d3":"52","explain":"s = ut + ½at² = (5 x 4) + (0.5 x 2 x 16) = 20 + 16 = 36 m.","formula":"s = ut + ½at²","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Leaving out the ut term, or leaving out the ½."},{"q":"On a speed-time graph a vehicle decelerates from 6 m/s to 0 m/s over 4 s. How far does it travel?","a":"12","unit":"m","d1":"24","d2":"0.75","d3":"10","explain":"The area is a triangle: ½ x base x height = 0.5 x 4 x 6 = 12 m.","formula":"distance = area under graph","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Using the rectangle area instead of the triangle."},{"q":"A walker covers 120 m in 15 s. What is the average speed?","a":"8","unit":"m/s","d1":"1800","d2":"0.125","d3":"105","explain":"Average speed = 120 / 15 = 8 m/s.","formula":"v = d/t","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying distance by time."},{"q":"A car accelerates from 0 to 20 m/s in 8 s. What is its acceleration?","a":"2.5","unit":"m/s²","d1":"160","d2":"0.4","d3":"12","explain":"a = (v - u)/t = 20 / 8 = 2.5 m/s².","formula":"a = (v - u)/t","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying speed by time."},{"q":"A car accelerates steadily from 0 to 20 m/s in 8 s. How far does it travel?","a":"80","unit":"m","d1":"160","d2":"1.25","d3":"28","explain":"For uniform acceleration the area is a triangle: ½ x 8 x 20 = 80 m.","formula":"distance = area under graph","topic":"motion","tier":2,"trap":"Using speed x time as if the speed were constant at 20 m/s."},{"q":"A car travels at 54 km/h for 20 s. How far does it travel?","a":"300","unit":"m","d1":"1080","d2":"15","d3":"540","explain":"Convert first: 54 / 3.6 = 15 m/s, then d = 15 x 20 = 300 m.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Using km/h directly with seconds."},{"q":"A trolley moves at 4 m/s for 10 s, then at 8 m/s for 5 s. What total distance does it cover?","a":"80","unit":"m","d1":"100","d2":"40","d3":"180","explain":"Distance is the sum of each stage: 40 + 40 = 80 m.","formula":"d = v1t1 + v2t2","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Swapping the speeds between the time intervals."},{"q":"A ball is thrown at 10 m/s and slows at 2 m/s² over 21 m. What is its final speed?","a":"4","unit":"m/s","d1":"184","d2":"-32","d3":"16","explain":"v² = u² + 2as = 100 - 84 = 16, so v = 4 m/s.","formula":"v² = u² + 2as","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Adding 2as when the acceleration opposes the motion, or reporting v²."},{"q":"A ball is thrown up at 8 m/s and decelerates at 2 m/s². How high is it after 3 s?","a":"15","unit":"m","d1":"33","d2":"24","d3":"9","explain":"s = ut + ½at² = 24 - 9 = 15 m, taking up as positive and a = -2 m/s².","formula":"s = ut + ½at²","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Making the deceleration term positive, which gives 33 m."},{"q":"A car does a 60 km journey out at 60 km/h and returns at 40 km/h. What is its average speed?","a":"48","unit":"km/h","d1":"50","d2":"24","d3":"25","explain":"Average speed uses total distance over total time: 120 km / 2.5 h = 48 km/h.","formula":"average speed = total distance / total time","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Taking the mean of the two speeds, which ignores the unequal times."},{"q":"A car at 20 m/s brakes at 5 m/s². What is its stopping distance?","a":"40","unit":"m","d1":"80","d2":"4","d3":"100","explain":"s = v²/(2a) = 400 / 10 = 40 m.","formula":"v² = u² + 2as","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting the 2 in the denominator, or using v/a."},{"q":"A driver reacts for 0.5 s at 20 m/s, then brakes at 5 m/s². What is the total stopping distance?","a":"50","unit":"m","d1":"40","d2":"10","d3":"14","explain":"Thinking distance 10 m plus braking distance 40 m gives 50 m.","formula":"stopping distance = thinking + braking","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Leaving out the thinking distance, or miscomputing the braking part."},{"q":"A vehicle's speed rises from 0 to 12 m/s in 4 s, then stays at 12 m/s for 6 s. What total distance is covered?","a":"96","unit":"m","d1":"120","d2":"24","d3":"144","explain":"Add the triangle (24 m) and the rectangle (72 m): 96 m.","formula":"distance = area under graph","topic":"motion","tier":3,"trap":"Using the full rectangle for the acceleration stage."},{"q":"A 4 kg mass is lifted 3 m. What is its gain in gravitational potential energy? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"117.6","unit":"J","d1":"12","d2":"39.2","d3":"235.2","explain":"GPE = mgh = 4 x 9.8 x 3 = 117.6 J.","formula":"GPE = mgh","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Leaving g out, or doubling the answer."},{"q":"A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s. What is its kinetic energy?","a":"9","unit":"J","d1":"18","d2":"3","d3":"6","explain":"KE = ½mv² = 0.5 x 2 x 9 = 9 J.","formula":"KE = ½mv²","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Forgetting the ½, or failing to square the speed."},{"q":"A 60 W lamp is on for 10 s. How much energy does it transfer?","a":"600","unit":"J","d1":"6","d2":"70","d3":"1200","explain":"Energy = power x time = 60 x 10 = 600 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing power by time."},{"q":"A 500 W heater runs for 4 minutes. How much energy does it transfer in kJ?","a":"120","unit":"kJ","d1":"2","d2":"120000","d3":"2000","explain":"Time in seconds is 240 s, so E = 500 x 240 = 120000 J = 120 kJ.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Using minutes instead of seconds, or forgetting the kilojoule conversion."},{"q":"What is the unit of energy?","a":"joule","unit":"","d1":"watt","d2":"newton","d3":"pascal","explain":"Energy is measured in joules; a watt is a rate of energy transfer.","formula":"","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing energy with power, force, or pressure."},{"q":"A motor does 300 J of work in 5 s. What is its power?","a":"60","unit":"W","d1":"1500","d2":"0.0167","d3":"295","explain":"Power = work / time = 300 / 5 = 60 W.","formula":"P = E/t","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying energy by time."},{"q":"A 12 V supply drives 2 A through a heater. What power is delivered?","a":"24","unit":"W","d1":"6","d2":"14","d3":"48","explain":"P = IV = 2 x 12 = 24 W.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing voltage by current."},{"q":"A force of 20 N moves a box 5 m in the direction of the force. What work is done?","a":"100","unit":"J","d1":"4","d2":"25","d3":"200","explain":"Work = force x distance = 20 x 5 = 100 J.","formula":"W = Fd","topic":"energy","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing force by distance."},{"q":"A 3 kg ball falls 2 m. What is its speed just before landing? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"6.26","unit":"m/s","d1":"39.2","d2":"4.43","d3":"19.6","explain":"GPE lost equals KE gained: ½v² = gh, so v = sqrt(2 x 9.8 x 2) = 6.26 m/s.","formula":"v = sqrt(2gh)","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Reporting v² as v, or leaving the 2 out of the square root."},{"q":"A 5 kg mass is lifted 2 m. What is its GPE gain? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"98","unit":"J","d1":"10","d2":"49","d3":"196","explain":"GPE = mgh = 5 x 9.8 x 2 = 98 J.","formula":"GPE = mgh","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Forgetting g, or doubling the product."},{"q":"A 4 kg object has 50 J of kinetic energy. What is its speed?","a":"5","unit":"m/s","d1":"12.5","d2":"6.25","d3":"25","explain":"Rearrange KE = ½mv²: v² = 2KE/m = 25, so v = 5 m/s.","formula":"KE = ½mv²","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Reporting v² as v, or inverting the factor of 2."},{"q":"A 1500 W kettle runs for 2 minutes. How much energy does it transfer?","a":"180000","unit":"J","d1":"3000","d2":"12.5","d3":"180","explain":"Time is 120 s, so E = 1500 x 120 = 180000 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Using 2 minutes as 2 seconds, or converting to kJ while labelling it J."},{"q":"A 10 kg mass rises 5 m. What is its GPE gain? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"490","unit":"J","d1":"50","d2":"98","d3":"980","explain":"GPE = mgh = 10 x 9.8 x 5 = 490 J.","formula":"GPE = mgh","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Using mass x height only."},{"q":"A machine does 600 J of work in 20 s. What is its power?","a":"30","unit":"W","d1":"12000","d2":"0.0333","d3":"580","explain":"P = E/t = 600 / 20 = 30 W.","formula":"P = E/t","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying work by time."},{"q":"A 12 V battery drives 0.5 A through a lamp. What power does the lamp use?","a":"6","unit":"W","d1":"24","d2":"12.5","d3":"12","explain":"P = IV = 0.5 x 12 = 6 W.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing voltage by current."},{"q":"A 0.5 kg ball moving at 6 m/s is stopped. How much kinetic energy is lost?","a":"9","unit":"J","d1":"18","d2":"1.5","d3":"3","explain":"KE = ½mv² = 0.5 x 0.5 x 36 = 9 J.","formula":"KE = ½mv²","topic":"energy","tier":2,"trap":"Using mass 0.5 as the result, or forgetting the ½."},{"q":"A 60 kg runner climbs 10 m. What is her GPE gain? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"5880","unit":"J","d1":"600","d2":"588","d3":"11760","explain":"GPE = mgh = 60 x 9.8 x 10 = 5880 J.","formula":"GPE = mgh","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Leaving g out, or doubling the product."},{"q":"A 2 kW heater runs for 3 minutes. How much energy does it transfer in kJ?","a":"360","unit":"kJ","d1":"6","d2":"360000","d3":"6000","explain":"2 kW = 2000 W and 3 min = 180 s, so E = 2000 x 180 = 360000 J = 360 kJ.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Using minutes for time, or kW as W."},{"q":"A 2 kg object falls 5 m. What is its speed at the bottom? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"9.9","unit":"m/s","d1":"98","d2":"7","d3":"49","explain":"v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(2 x 9.8 x 5) = sqrt(98) = 9.9 m/s.","formula":"v = sqrt(2gh)","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting v² as v, or omitting the factor 2."},{"q":"A 5 kg box is pushed 4 m along a floor against 10 N of friction. How much work is done against friction?","a":"40","unit":"J","d1":"200","d2":"2.5","d3":"20","explain":"Work against friction = friction x distance = 10 x 4 = 40 J.","formula":"W = Fd","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Multiplying by mass, which does not appear in the formula."},{"q":"A 1.5 kW motor runs for 40 s. How much energy does it transfer?","a":"60000","unit":"J","d1":"37.5","d2":"60","d3":"1540","explain":"E = Pt = 1500 x 40 = 60000 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing power by time, or converting to kJ while labelling it J."},{"q":"A 0.2 kg ball moving at 10 m/s has how much kinetic energy?","a":"10","unit":"J","d1":"20","d2":"1","d3":"2","explain":"KE = ½mv² = 0.5 x 0.2 x 100 = 10 J.","formula":"KE = ½mv²","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting the ½, or failing to square the speed."},{"q":"A 500 N crate is lifted 2 m by a motor in 4 s. What is the motor's useful power?","a":"250","unit":"W","d1":"1000","d2":"2000","d3":"4000","explain":"Work = 500 x 2 = 1000 J, so P = 1000 / 4 = 250 W.","formula":"P = E/t","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the work rather than the power, or inverting the division."},{"q":"A 0.4 kg ball is dropped from 1.8 m. What is its speed just before impact? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"5.94","unit":"m/s","d1":"35.28","d2":"4.2","d3":"17.64","explain":"v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(35.28) = 5.94 m/s.","formula":"v = sqrt(2gh)","topic":"energy","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting v², or omitting the 2."},{"q":"A current of 2 A flows for 30 s. How much charge passes?","a":"60","unit":"C","d1":"0.0667","d2":"32","d3":"120","explain":"Charge = current x time = 2 x 30 = 60 C.","formula":"Q = It","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing current by time."},{"q":"A charge of 12 C flows in 3 s. What is the current?","a":"4","unit":"A","d1":"36","d2":"0.25","d3":"9","explain":"I = Q/t = 12 / 3 = 4 A.","formula":"Q = It","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying charge by time."},{"q":"A 6 V battery is connected to a 3 ohm resistor. What current flows?","a":"2","unit":"A","d1":"18","d2":"0.5","d3":"3","explain":"I = V/R = 6 / 3 = 2 A.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying voltage by resistance."},{"q":"A current of 0.5 A flows through a 20 ohm resistor. What is the voltage across it?","a":"10","unit":"V","d1":"0.025","d2":"40","d3":"20.5","explain":"V = IR = 0.5 x 20 = 10 V.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying."},{"q":"Two 4 ohm resistors are connected in series. What is the total resistance?","a":"8","unit":"Ω","d1":"2","d2":"4","d3":"16","explain":"Series resistances add: 4 + 4 = 8 ohms.","formula":"R = R1 + R2","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Using the parallel rule, which gives 2 ohms."},{"q":"What is the unit of electrical resistance?","a":"ohm","unit":"","d1":"volt","d2":"ampere","d3":"coulomb","explain":"Resistance is measured in ohms; voltage in volts and current in amperes.","formula":"","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing resistance with voltage or current."},{"q":"A 3 V cell is connected across a 1.5 ohm resistor. What current flows?","a":"2","unit":"A","d1":"4.5","d2":"0.5","d3":"1.5","explain":"I = V/R = 3 / 1.5 = 2 A.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying voltage by resistance."},{"q":"A charge of 60 C passes through a lamp in 20 s. What is the current?","a":"3","unit":"A","d1":"1200","d2":"0.333","d3":"40","explain":"I = Q/t = 60 / 20 = 3 A.","formula":"Q = It","topic":"electricity","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying charge by time."},{"q":"A 12 V supply drives 3 A. What is the resistance of the circuit?","a":"4","unit":"Ω","d1":"36","d2":"0.25","d3":"9","explain":"Rearrange V = IR to R = V/I = 12 / 3 = 4 ohms.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying voltage by current."},{"q":"Two 6 ohm resistors are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance?","a":"3","unit":"Ω","d1":"12","d2":"6","d3":"1.5","explain":"For two equal resistors in parallel, R = R/2 = 3 ohms.","formula":"1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Adding them as if in series, which gives 12 ohms."},{"q":"A 9 V battery drives 0.3 A through a bulb. What is the bulb's resistance?","a":"30","unit":"Ω","d1":"2.7","d2":"0.0333","d3":"8.7","explain":"R = V/I = 9 / 0.3 = 30 ohms.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying voltage by current."},{"q":"A 4 ohm and a 12 ohm resistor are in series across 8 V. What current flows?","a":"0.5","unit":"A","d1":"2","d2":"0.667","d3":"128","explain":"Total resistance is 16 ohms, so I = V/R = 8 / 16 = 0.5 A.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Using only one resistor's resistance."},{"q":"A 2 A current flows for 5 minutes. How much charge passes?","a":"600","unit":"C","d1":"10","d2":"100","d3":"60","explain":"Time is 300 s, so Q = It = 2 x 300 = 600 C.","formula":"Q = It","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Using minutes instead of seconds."},{"q":"A 6 ohm and a 3 ohm resistor are in parallel. What is the total resistance?","a":"2","unit":"Ω","d1":"9","d2":"18","d3":"4.5","explain":"1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2, so R = 2 ohms.","formula":"1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Adding them in series, which gives 9 ohms."},{"q":"A 24 V supply drives 4 A through a motor. What is its resistance?","a":"6","unit":"Ω","d1":"96","d2":"0.167","d3":"20","explain":"R = V/I = 24 / 4 = 6 ohms.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying voltage by current."},{"q":"A 2 A current flows through a 5 ohm resistor. What voltage is across it?","a":"10","unit":"V","d1":"0.4","d2":"2.5","d3":"7","explain":"V = IR = 2 x 5 = 10 V.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing resistance by current."},{"q":"A 12 V supply drives 0.5 A through a bulb for 2 minutes. How much energy is transferred?","a":"720","unit":"J","d1":"12","d2":"6","d3":"0.72","explain":"Power is 6 W, and time is 120 s, so E = Pt = 6 x 120 = 720 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Using minutes as seconds, or reporting kJ while labelling it J."},{"q":"A 12 ohm and a 4 ohm resistor are in series across 16 V. What is the voltage across the 12 ohm resistor?","a":"12","unit":"V","d1":"4","d2":"16","d3":"1.33","explain":"Total R is 16 ohms so I = 1 A; across 12 ohms, V = IR = 12 V.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Swapping the resistor values, which gives the 4 ohm voltage."},{"q":"A 6 ohm and a 3 ohm resistor are in series across 18 V. What current flows?","a":"2","unit":"A","d1":"3","d2":"6","d3":"162","explain":"Total resistance is 9 ohms, so I = 18 / 9 = 2 A.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Using one resistor's value only."},{"q":"A 3 ohm and a 6 ohm resistor are in parallel. What is the total resistance?","a":"2","unit":"Ω","d1":"9","d2":"4.5","d3":"18","explain":"1/R = 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2, so R = 2 ohms.","formula":"1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Adding them as if in series, which gives 9 ohms."},{"q":"A 60 W bulb is on for 5 hours. How much energy does it use in kWh?","a":"0.3","unit":"kWh","d1":"300","d2":"1080000","d3":"3","explain":"Energy in kWh = power in kW x time in h = 0.06 x 5 = 0.3 kWh.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Leaving the power in watts, or converting to joules instead of kWh."},{"q":"A 9 V battery supplies 0.2 A for 50 s. How much energy is transferred?","a":"90","unit":"J","d1":"1.8","d2":"0.09","d3":"9","explain":"Power is 1.8 W, so E = 1.8 x 50 = 90 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Using the power as the energy, or losing a factor of 10."},{"q":"Two resistors in series total 10 ohms and carry 0.4 A. What is the supply voltage?","a":"4","unit":"V","d1":"25","d2":"0.04","d3":"10.4","explain":"V = IR = 0.4 x 10 = 4 V.","formula":"V = IR","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing resistance by current."},{"q":"A 12 V supply drives 0.25 A through a lamp for 40 s. How much charge passes?","a":"10","unit":"C","d1":"3","d2":"0.0063","d3":"120","explain":"Charge depends only on current and time: Q = It = 0.25 x 40 = 10 C.","formula":"Q = It","topic":"electricity","tier":3,"trap":"Multiplying by voltage as well, which gives energy not charge."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 4 Hz and wavelength 3 m. What is its speed?","a":"12","unit":"m/s","d1":"1.33","d2":"7","d3":"24","explain":"Wave speed = frequency x wavelength = 4 x 3 = 12 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing frequency by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave travels at 20 m/s with frequency 5 Hz. What is its wavelength?","a":"4","unit":"m","d1":"100","d2":"0.25","d3":"15","explain":"λ = v/f = 20 / 5 = 4 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying speed by frequency."},{"q":"A sound wave has frequency 50 Hz. What is its period?","a":"0.02","unit":"s","d1":"50","d2":"0.002","d3":"0.5","explain":"Period = 1/f = 1 / 50 = 0.02 s.","formula":"T = 1/f","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Reporting the frequency, or a factor-of-ten error."},{"q":"A wave has period 0.2 s. What is its frequency?","a":"5","unit":"Hz","d1":"0.2","d2":"10","d3":"2","explain":"f = 1/T = 1 / 0.2 = 5 Hz.","formula":"f = 1/T","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Reporting the period, or taking the reciprocal of 0.02 instead."},{"q":"What is the unit of frequency?","a":"hertz","unit":"","d1":"second","d2":"metre","d3":"joule","explain":"Frequency counts oscillations per second, measured in hertz.","formula":"","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing frequency with period, which is in seconds."},{"q":"A wave travels 60 m in 6 s. What is its speed?","a":"10","unit":"m/s","d1":"360","d2":"0.1","d3":"54","explain":"Speed = distance / time = 60 / 6 = 10 m/s.","formula":"v = d/t","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying distance by time."},{"q":"A wave with wavelength 0.5 m passes a point 4 times each second. What is its speed?","a":"2","unit":"m/s","d1":"8","d2":"4.5","d3":"4","explain":"v = fλ = 4 x 0.5 = 2 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing frequency by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 200 Hz and wavelength 1.5 m. What is its speed?","a":"300","unit":"m/s","d1":"133.33","d2":"201.5","d3":"600","explain":"v = fλ = 200 x 1.5 = 300 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying."},{"q":"A wave travels at 340 m/s with wavelength 0.85 m. What is its frequency?","a":"400","unit":"Hz","d1":"289","d2":"0.0025","d3":"339.15","explain":"f = v/λ = 340 / 0.85 = 400 Hz.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying speed by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 25 Hz. What is its period?","a":"0.04","unit":"s","d1":"25","d2":"0.4","d3":"2.5","explain":"T = 1/f = 1 / 25 = 0.04 s.","formula":"T = 1/f","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Reporting the frequency, or a factor-of-ten error."},{"q":"A wave has period 0.04 s. What is its frequency?","a":"25","unit":"Hz","d1":"0.04","d2":"4","d3":"40","explain":"f = 1/T = 1 / 0.04 = 25 Hz.","formula":"f = 1/T","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Reporting the period, or a factor-of-ten error."},{"q":"A wave travels 15 m in 5 s and has wavelength 0.75 m. What is its frequency?","a":"4","unit":"Hz","d1":"2.25","d2":"0.25","d3":"20","explain":"Speed is 3 m/s, so f = v/λ = 3 / 0.75 = 4 Hz.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Forgetting to divide the distance by the time first."},{"q":"A sound wave of frequency 100 Hz travels at 340 m/s. What is its wavelength?","a":"3.4","unit":"m","d1":"34000","d2":"0.294","d3":"240","explain":"λ = v/f = 340 / 100 = 3.4 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying speed by frequency."},{"q":"A wave has speed 8 m/s and wavelength 2 m. What is its frequency?","a":"4","unit":"Hz","d1":"16","d2":"0.25","d3":"6","explain":"f = v/λ = 8 / 2 = 4 Hz.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying speed by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 12 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m. What is its speed?","a":"6","unit":"m/s","d1":"24","d2":"12.5","d3":"12","explain":"v = fλ = 12 x 0.5 = 6 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing frequency by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has period 0.02 s and wavelength 0.1 m. What is its speed?","a":"5","unit":"m/s","d1":"0.002","d2":"0.004","d3":"500","explain":"Frequency is 1/0.02 = 50 Hz, so v = fλ = 50 x 0.1 = 5 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":2,"trap":"Using the period as the frequency."},{"q":"A wave travels 90 m in 3 s and has frequency 20 Hz. What is its wavelength?","a":"1.5","unit":"m","d1":"600","d2":"0.667","d3":"30","explain":"Speed is 30 m/s, so λ = v/f = 30 / 20 = 1.5 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the speed instead of the wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has wavelength 0.25 m and frequency 400 Hz. What is its speed?","a":"100","unit":"m/s","d1":"1600","d2":"400.25","d3":"200","explain":"v = fλ = 400 x 0.25 = 100 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing frequency by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has period 0.005 s. What is its frequency?","a":"200","unit":"Hz","d1":"0.005","d2":"20","d3":"2000","explain":"f = 1/T = 1 / 0.005 = 200 Hz.","formula":"f = 1/T","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"A factor-of-ten error in the reciprocal."},{"q":"A wave travels at 24 m/s and has period 0.4 s. What is its wavelength?","a":"9.6","unit":"m","d1":"60","d2":"0.0167","d3":"24.4","explain":"Frequency is 1/0.4 = 2.5 Hz, so λ = v/f = 24 / 2.5 = 9.6 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing speed by the period instead of converting the period to frequency."},{"q":"A wave travels 250 m in 5 s and has frequency 10 Hz. What is its wavelength?","a":"5","unit":"m","d1":"500","d2":"0.2","d3":"50","explain":"Speed is 50 m/s, so λ = 50 / 10 = 5 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the speed rather than the wavelength."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 250 Hz and wavelength 1.2 m. What is its speed?","a":"300","unit":"m/s","d1":"208.33","d2":"251.2","d3":"600","explain":"v = fλ = 250 x 1.2 = 300 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing frequency by wavelength."},{"q":"A wave with period 0.01 s has wavelength 3.4 m. What is its speed?","a":"340","unit":"m/s","d1":"0.034","d2":"0.34","d3":"29.41","explain":"Frequency is 100 Hz, so v = 100 x 3.4 = 340 m/s.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Using the period as the frequency."},{"q":"A wave has speed 6 m/s and period 0.25 s. What is its wavelength?","a":"1.5","unit":"m","d1":"24","d2":"0.0417","d3":"6.25","explain":"Frequency is 1/0.25 = 4 Hz, so λ = 6 / 4 = 1.5 m.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"waves","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing speed by period, which is not the wavelength formula."},{"q":"A ray of light hits a mirror at 30° to the normal. At what angle to the normal is it reflected?","a":"30","unit":"°","d1":"60","d2":"15","d3":"150","explain":"The law of reflection says the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence, so 30°.","formula":"angle i = angle r","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Measuring from the mirror surface instead of the normal, which gives 60°."},{"q":"A ray strikes a mirror at 40° to the normal. What is the angle between the incident and reflected rays?","a":"80","unit":"°","d1":"40","d2":"100","d3":"140","explain":"Both rays make 40° with the normal on opposite sides, so the angle between them is 80°.","formula":"angle i = angle r","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Reporting the angle of incidence rather than the angle between the rays."},{"q":"How far does light travel in one second? (speed of light = 3 × 10^8 m/s)","a":"300000000","unit":"m","d1":"300000","d2":"30000000","d3":"3000000000","explain":"Distance = speed × time = 3 × 10^8 × 1 = 300000000 m.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Losing or gaining a factor of ten in the power of ten."},{"q":"A red filter is placed in front of a white lamp. What colour is the light that passes through?","a":"red","unit":"","d1":"white","d2":"black","d3":"green","explain":"A colour filter transmits its own colour and absorbs the rest, so only red passes.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Thinking the filter adds colour to white light rather than removing it."},{"q":"White light passes through a glass prism. What is the spreading of colours called?","a":"dispersion","unit":"","d1":"reflection","d2":"absorption","d3":"diffraction","explain":"Different colours refract by different amounts, so the white light disperses into a spectrum.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing dispersion with reflection or absorption."},{"q":"Which surface makes a clear image by reflection: a smooth mirror or rough paper?","a":"smooth mirror","unit":"","d1":"rough paper","d2":"both equally","d3":"neither","explain":"A smooth surface reflects rays in one direction (specular reflection), so an image forms.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Thinking rough paper reflects no light at all."},{"q":"State the law of reflection for a ray hitting a mirror.","a":"angle of incidence equals angle of reflection","unit":"","d1":"angle of incidence equals 90°","d2":"angle of refraction equals angle of incidence","d3":"angle of reflection is always 45°","explain":"The incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane, and i = r.","formula":"angle i = angle r","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing reflection with refraction, or inventing a fixed angle."},{"q":"Light from a small lamp passes a solid card and forms a sharp shadow. What does this show?","a":"light travels in straight lines","unit":"","d1":"light bends around corners","d2":"light is a wave only","d3":"light can pass through card","explain":"Sharp shadows occur because light travels in straight lines and cannot bend around the card.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":1,"trap":"Believing light bends around every obstacle."},{"q":"A convex lens of focal length 10 cm has an object 20 cm away. Where is the image?","a":"20","unit":"cm","d1":"10","d2":"30","d3":"5","explain":"1/f = 1/u + 1/v, so 1/v = 1/10 - 1/20 = 1/20 and v = 20 cm.","formula":"1/f = 1/u + 1/v","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Adding u and f instead of using the lens formula."},{"q":"An object 2 cm tall forms an image 6 cm tall. What is the magnification?","a":"3","unit":"","d1":"0.333","d2":"12","d3":"4","explain":"Magnification = image height / object height = 6 / 2 = 3.","formula":"m = hi/ho","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the ratio, or adding the heights."},{"q":"Light travels at 3 × 10^8 m/s in air and 2 × 10^8 m/s in glass. What is the refractive index of the glass?","a":"1.5","unit":"","d1":"0.667","d2":"5","d3":"6","explain":"n = c/v = (3 × 10^8) / (2 × 10^8) = 1.5.","formula":"n = c/v","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the ratio to get v/c."},{"q":"A material has refractive index 1.5. What is the speed of light in it? (c = 3 × 10^8 m/s)","a":"200000000","unit":"m/s","d1":"450000000","d2":"150000000","d3":"300000000","explain":"v = c/n = (3 × 10^8) / 1.5 = 2 × 10^8 m/s.","formula":"n = c/v","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying by n instead of dividing."},{"q":"A convex lens of focal length 10 cm has an object 15 cm away. Where is the image?","a":"30","unit":"cm","d1":"6","d2":"25","d3":"15","explain":"1/v = 1/10 - 1/15 = 1/30, so v = 30 cm.","formula":"1/f = 1/u + 1/v","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Subtracting in the wrong order, which gives 1/30 with the wrong sign."},{"q":"A convex lens forms an image 30 cm from the lens from an object 15 cm away. What is the magnification?","a":"2","unit":"","d1":"0.5","d2":"45","d3":"15","explain":"m = v/u = 30 / 15 = 2.","formula":"m = v/u","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Using u/v, which gives the reciprocal."},{"q":"Light of wavelength 600 nm has what frequency? (c = 3 × 10^8 m/s)","a":"500","unit":"THz","d1":"200","d2":"180","d3":"5000","explain":"f = c/λ = (3 × 10^8) / (600 × 10^-9) = 5 × 10^14 Hz = 500 THz.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Forgetting to convert nanometres to metres."},{"q":"Light of frequency 400 THz has what wavelength? (c = 3 × 10^8 m/s)","a":"750","unit":"nm","d1":"120","d2":"1333","d3":"75","explain":"λ = c/f = (3 × 10^8) / (4 × 10^14) = 7.5 × 10^-7 m = 750 nm.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Leaving the answer in metres and calling it nanometres."},{"q":"A convex lens forms a real image. Which describes it?","a":"inverted","unit":"","d1":"upright","d2":"virtual","d3":"always smaller","explain":"A real image from a single convex lens is inverted and can be projected on a screen.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming every image is upright like a magnifying glass."},{"q":"What does a concave (diverging) lens do to parallel rays?","a":"spreads them out","unit":"","d1":"brings them to a focus","d2":"reflects them back","d3":"absorbs them","explain":"A concave lens diverges parallel rays, so they appear to come from a virtual focus.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":2,"trap":"Confusing a concave lens with a convex converging lens."},{"q":"The refractive index of glass is 1.5. What is the critical angle for glass to air?","a":"41.8","unit":"°","d1":"30","d2":"48.6","d3":"60","explain":"sin c = 1/n = 1/1.5, so c = sin⁻¹(0.667) = 41.8°.","formula":"sin c = 1/n","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Using n as the sine, or forgetting the inverse sine."},{"q":"A convex lens of focal length 10 cm has an object 30 cm away. Where is the image?","a":"15","unit":"cm","d1":"7.5","d2":"20","d3":"40","explain":"1/v = 1/10 - 1/30 = 2/30, so v = 15 cm.","formula":"1/f = 1/u + 1/v","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Adding the reciprocals instead of subtracting them."},{"q":"A convex lens has an object 30 cm away and forms an image 15 cm away. What is the magnification?","a":"0.5","unit":"","d1":"2","d2":"45","d3":"0.33","explain":"m = v/u = 15 / 30 = 0.5, so the image is half size.","formula":"m = v/u","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Inverting the ratio, which gives 2."},{"q":"A 2 m tall object stands 4 m from a pinhole camera whose screen is 0.5 m behind the hole. How tall is the image?","a":"0.25","unit":"m","d1":"16","d2":"1","d3":"2","explain":"Similar triangles give hi/ho = screen distance/object distance, so hi = 2 × 0.5/4 = 0.25 m.","formula":"hi/ho = v/u","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Inverting the distance ratio."},{"q":"Light of frequency 500 THz enters glass where its speed is 2 × 10^8 m/s. What is its wavelength in the glass?","a":"400","unit":"nm","d1":"600","d2":"250","d3":"1000","explain":"Frequency does not change, so λ = v/f = (2 × 10^8)/(5 × 10^14) = 4 × 10^-7 m = 400 nm.","formula":"v = fλ","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Using the speed of light in air instead of in the glass."},{"q":"Total internal reflection can happen when light travels from","a":"glass to air","unit":"","d1":"air to glass","d2":"air to water","d3":"vacuum to glass","explain":"Total internal reflection needs the ray to go from a denser to a less dense medium, such as glass to air.","formula":"","topic":"light","tier":3,"trap":"Reversing the direction, which sends the ray into the denser medium."},{"q":"How much energy raises the temperature of 2 kg of water by 10 °C? (c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"84000","unit":"J","d1":"20","d2":"8400","d3":"168000","explain":"E = mcΔT = 2 × 4200 × 10 = 84000 J.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Leaving out the specific heat capacity, or doubling the product."},{"q":"How much energy heats 0.5 kg of aluminium by 20 °C? (c = 900 J/kg°C)","a":"9000","unit":"J","d1":"10","d2":"450","d3":"18000","explain":"E = mcΔT = 0.5 × 900 × 20 = 9000 J.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Using the mass without the specific heat capacity."},{"q":"Convert 25 °C to kelvin.","a":"298","unit":"K","d1":"-248","d2":"25","d3":"198","explain":"K = °C + 273, so 25 + 273 = 298 K.","formula":"K = °C + 273","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Subtracting 273, which converts the other way."},{"q":"Convert 300 K to degrees Celsius.","a":"27","unit":"°C","d1":"573","d2":"300","d3":"-73","explain":"°C = K - 273, so 300 - 273 = 27 °C.","formula":"°C = K - 273","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Adding 273 instead of subtracting."},{"q":"What is the unit of specific heat capacity?","a":"J/kg°C","unit":"","d1":"J","d2":"W","d3":"°C","explain":"Specific heat capacity is energy per kilogram per degree, measured in J/kg°C.","formula":"","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing it with energy or with temperature."},{"q":"How much energy melts 0.5 kg of ice at 0 °C? (latent heat of fusion = 334000 J/kg)","a":"167000","unit":"J","d1":"334000","d2":"668000","d3":"1670","explain":"E = mL = 0.5 × 334000 = 167000 J.","formula":"E = mL","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing by the latent heat, or forgetting the mass."},{"q":"Which method transfers heat through a solid metal bar?","a":"conduction","unit":"","d1":"convection","d2":"radiation","d3":"evaporation","explain":"In a solid, particles pass energy along by vibration, which is conduction.","formula":"","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Choosing convection, which needs a moving fluid."},{"q":"Which surface is the best emitter of thermal radiation?","a":"black matt","unit":"","d1":"white shiny","d2":"silver polished","d3":"clear glass","explain":"Black matt surfaces absorb and emit radiation best; shiny surfaces reflect it.","formula":"","topic":"heat","tier":1,"trap":"Thinking shiny surfaces emit most because they look bright."},{"q":"How much energy raises 0.5 kg of water by 40 °C? (c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"84000","unit":"J","d1":"20","d2":"2100","d3":"168000","explain":"E = mcΔT = 0.5 × 4200 × 40 = 84000 J.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Leaving out the mass, or doubling the product."},{"q":"A 2 kg block gains 8400 J and rises 4 °C. What is its specific heat capacity?","a":"1050","unit":"J/kg°C","d1":"4200","d2":"67200","d3":"2100","explain":"Rearrange E = mcΔT to c = E/(mΔT) = 8400/(2 × 4) = 1050 J/kg°C.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing by mass only, or multiplying instead of dividing."},{"q":"A 0.2 kg metal block gains 1800 J and rises 10 °C. What is its specific heat capacity?","a":"900","unit":"J/kg°C","d1":"3600","d2":"9000","d3":"36","explain":"c = E/(mΔT) = 1800/(0.2 × 10) = 900 J/kg°C.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing by mass only and forgetting the temperature change."},{"q":"How much energy turns 0.2 kg of water at 100 °C into steam? (latent heat of vaporisation = 2.26 × 10^6 J/kg)","a":"452000","unit":"J","d1":"452","d2":"2260000","d3":"904000","explain":"E = mL = 0.2 × 2.26 × 10^6 = 452000 J.","formula":"E = mL","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Using the latent heat of fusion, or losing a factor of 1000."},{"q":"A 2 kg block of water at 20 °C is heated to 60 °C. How much energy is needed? (c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"336000","unit":"J","d1":"504000","d2":"168000","d3":"672000","explain":"The change is 60 - 20 = 40 °C, so E = 2 × 4200 × 40 = 336000 J.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Using the final temperature instead of the change."},{"q":"1 kg of water at 80 °C is mixed with 1 kg of water at 20 °C. What is the final temperature?","a":"50","unit":"°C","d1":"60","d2":"80","d3":"20","explain":"Equal masses of the same liquid end at the mean temperature, 50 °C.","formula":"heat lost = heat gained","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Subtracting the temperatures instead of averaging."},{"q":"2 kg of water at 80 °C is mixed with 1 kg of water at 20 °C. What is the final temperature?","a":"60","unit":"°C","d1":"50","d2":"40","d3":"180","explain":"Heat lost equals heat gained, so T = (2×80 + 1×20)/3 = 60 °C.","formula":"heat lost = heat gained","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Taking the simple mean, which ignores the unequal masses."},{"q":"A liquid cools from 90 °C to 30 °C in 10 minutes. What is its average cooling rate?","a":"6","unit":"°C/min","d1":"9","d2":"3","d3":"12","explain":"The drop is 60 °C over 10 min, so the rate is 6 °C/min.","formula":"rate = ΔT/t","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Using the initial temperature rather than the change."},{"q":"During melting, what happens to the temperature of the substance?","a":"stays constant","unit":"","d1":"rises steadily","d2":"falls steadily","d3":"doubles","explain":"Energy supplied during a change of state breaks bonds, so the temperature stays constant.","formula":"","topic":"heat","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming temperature always rises while energy is added."},{"q":"A 2 kW heater warms 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 100 °C. How long does it take? (c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"336","unit":"s","d1":"672","d2":"672000","d3":"420","explain":"Energy needed is 672000 J, so t = E/P = 672000/2000 = 336 s.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Using the final temperature rather than the 80 °C rise."},{"q":"A 500 g block of water rises from 20 °C to 60 °C in 120 s. What average power heated it? (c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"700","unit":"W","d1":"84000","d2":"1050","d3":"10080000","explain":"Energy is 84000 J, so P = E/t = 84000/120 = 700 W.","formula":"P = E/t","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Using 500 g as 500 kg, or using the final temperature."},{"q":"0.2 kg of ice at 0 °C melts and the water then warms to 20 °C. How much energy is needed in total? (L = 334000 J/kg, c = 4200 J/kg°C)","a":"83600","unit":"J","d1":"66800","d2":"16800","d3":"150800","explain":"Melting takes 66800 J and warming takes 16800 J, giving 83600 J.","formula":"E = mL + mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting the melting stage, or using the latent heat twice."},{"q":"A metal block of mass 0.5 kg absorbs 9000 J and rises 20 °C. What is its specific heat capacity?","a":"900","unit":"J/kg°C","d1":"90000","d2":"18000","d3":"360000","explain":"c = E/(mΔT) = 9000/(0.5 × 20) = 900 J/kg°C.","formula":"E = mcΔT","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing by mass alone."},{"q":"A 3 kW heater runs for 5 minutes. How much energy does it supply?","a":"900000","unit":"J","d1":"15000","d2":"900","d3":"10","explain":"5 min is 300 s, so E = 3000 × 300 = 900000 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Using minutes rather than seconds."},{"q":"A house loses 6000 J each second through a wall. How much energy is lost in one hour?","a":"21600000","unit":"J","d1":"360000","d2":"21600","d3":"1.67","explain":"One hour is 3600 s, so E = 6000 × 3600 = 21600000 J.","formula":"E = Pt","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Using 60 s for one hour."},{"q":"Two identical kettles heat equal masses of water; one is insulated, one is not. Which reaches 100 °C first?","a":"insulated kettle","unit":"","d1":"uninsulated kettle","d2":"both together","d3":"neither","explain":"The insulated kettle loses less energy to the surroundings, so more energy goes into the water.","formula":"","topic":"heat","tier":3,"trap":"Assuming insulation changes the energy supplied by the heater."},{"q":"A force of 100 N acts on an area of 0.5 m². What is the pressure?","a":"200","unit":"Pa","d1":"50","d2":"0.005","d3":"100.5","explain":"Pressure = force/area = 100/0.5 = 200 Pa.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying force by area."},{"q":"A force of 500 N acts on 0.2 m². What is the pressure?","a":"2500","unit":"Pa","d1":"100","d2":"0.0004","d3":"499.8","explain":"P = F/A = 500/0.2 = 2500 Pa.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying instead of dividing."},{"q":"What is the unit of pressure?","a":"pascal","unit":"","d1":"newton","d2":"joule","d3":"kilogram","explain":"Pressure is force per area, measured in pascals (N/m²).","formula":"","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing pressure with force."},{"q":"What is the pressure due to 2 m of water? (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"19600","unit":"Pa","d1":"2000","d2":"9800","d3":"39200","explain":"P = ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × 2 = 19600 Pa.","formula":"P = ρgh","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Leaving out g, or doubling the product."},{"q":"Atmospheric pressure at sea level is about how many kilopascals?","a":"101","unit":"kPa","d1":"1.01","d2":"1010","d3":"10.1","explain":"Standard atmospheric pressure is about 101 kPa, a value worth remembering as roughly 100 kPa. No formula is needed.","formula":"","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing the value in Pa with kPa, or moving the decimal point."},{"q":"A pressure of 400 Pa acts on 0.5 m². What force does it produce?","a":"200","unit":"N","d1":"800","d2":"0.0013","d3":"400.5","explain":"Rearrange P = F/A to F = PA = 400 × 0.5 = 200 N.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing pressure by area."},{"q":"A force of 200 N acts on 0.5 m². What is the pressure?","a":"400","unit":"Pa","d1":"100","d2":"0.0025","d3":"200.5","explain":"P = F/A = 200/0.5 = 400 Pa.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying force by area."},{"q":"A block weighs 60 N and rests on a face of area 0.03 m². What pressure does it exert?","a":"2000","unit":"Pa","d1":"1.8","d2":"0.0005","d3":"59.97","explain":"P = F/A = 60/0.03 = 2000 Pa.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":1,"trap":"Multiplying force by area."},{"q":"What is the pressure 5 m below the surface of water? (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"49000","unit":"Pa","d1":"5000","d2":"9800","d3":"98000","explain":"P = ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × 5 = 49000 Pa.","formula":"P = ρgh","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Leaving out the depth or g."},{"q":"A pressure of 500 Pa acts on 0.2 m². What force is produced?","a":"100","unit":"N","d1":"2500","d2":"0.0004","d3":"500.2","explain":"F = PA = 500 × 0.2 = 100 N.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing pressure by area."},{"q":"A force of 300 N produces a pressure of 1500 Pa. What area does it act on?","a":"0.2","unit":"m²","d1":"450000","d2":"5","d3":"1800","explain":"A = F/P = 300/1500 = 0.2 m².","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying force by pressure."},{"q":"A hydraulic press has a small piston of area 0.01 m² and a large piston of area 0.5 m². A force of 100 N on the small piston produces what force on the large one?","a":"5000","unit":"N","d1":"2","d2":"50","d3":"1","explain":"Pressure is the same throughout: F2 = F1 × A2/A1 = 100 × 50 = 5000 N.","formula":"F1/A1 = F2/A2","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the area ratio."},{"q":"A force of 50 N acts on a small piston of area 0.1 m². The connected large piston has area 0.4 m². What force does it feel?","a":"200","unit":"N","d1":"12.5","d2":"20","d3":"5","explain":"F2 = F1 × A2/A1 = 50 × 4 = 200 N.","formula":"F1/A1 = F2/A2","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the area ratio."},{"q":"A gas at 100 kPa occupies 2 m³. It is compressed to 1 m³ at constant temperature. What is the new pressure?","a":"200","unit":"kPa","d1":"50","d2":"100","d3":"400","explain":"Boyle's law: P1V1 = P2V2, so P2 = 100 × 2/1 = 200 kPa.","formula":"P1V1 = P2V2","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying by the new volume instead of dividing."},{"q":"A gas at 200 kPa occupies 0.5 m³. It expands to 1.0 m³ at constant temperature. What is the new pressure?","a":"100","unit":"kPa","d1":"400","d2":"200","d3":"50","explain":"P2 = P1V1/V2 = 200 × 0.5/1.0 = 100 kPa.","formula":"P1V1 = P2V2","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming pressure doubles when volume doubles."},{"q":"A 2 kg block has a volume of 0.5 m³. What is its density?","a":"4","unit":"kg/m³","d1":"1","d2":"0.25","d3":"2.5","explain":"Density = mass/volume = 2/0.5 = 4 kg/m³.","formula":"ρ = m/V","topic":"pressure","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying mass by volume."},{"q":"What is the pressure 10 m below the surface of water? (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"98000","unit":"Pa","d1":"10000","d2":"9800","d3":"196000","explain":"P = ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × 10 = 98000 Pa.","formula":"P = ρgh","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Leaving out g, or doubling the product."},{"q":"A nail carries a force of 50 N on a tip area of 0.0001 m². What pressure does the tip exert?","a":"500000","unit":"Pa","d1":"0.005","d2":"0","d3":"50000","explain":"P = F/A = 50/0.0001 = 500000 Pa.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Losing a power of ten in the area."},{"q":"A gas occupies 100 cm³ at 100 kPa. It is compressed to 50 cm³ at constant temperature. What is its new pressure?","a":"200","unit":"kPa","d1":"50","d2":"400","d3":"100","explain":"P2 = P1V1/V2 = 100 × 100/50 = 200 kPa; the units cancel as long as both volumes match.","formula":"P1V1 = P2V2","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Halving the pressure when the volume halves."},{"q":"A pressure of 100000 Pa acts on 0.002 m². What force is produced?","a":"200","unit":"N","d1":"50000000","d2":"0","d3":"2000","explain":"F = PA = 100000 × 0.002 = 200 N.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying, or a factor-of-ten error."},{"q":"How high would a water column be to balance atmospheric pressure? (P = 101000 Pa, ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²)","a":"10.31","unit":"m","d1":"101","d2":"989800000","d3":"0.097","explain":"h = P/(ρg) = 101000/(1000 × 9.8) = 10.3 m.","formula":"P = ρgh","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Forgetting to divide by g."},{"q":"A block of mass 3 kg has density 1500 kg/m³. What is its volume?","a":"0.002","unit":"m³","d1":"4500","d2":"500","d3":"1503","explain":"V = m/ρ = 3/1500 = 0.002 m³.","formula":"ρ = m/V","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Multiplying mass by density."},{"q":"The same 500 N force acts on a wide snowshoe and a narrow boot. Which exerts the greater pressure?","a":"the boot","unit":"","d1":"the snowshoe","d2":"both the same","d3":"neither","explain":"Pressure = force/area, so the smaller area of the boot gives the greater pressure.","formula":"P = F/A","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Thinking the larger area gives the larger pressure."},{"q":"A barometer reads 760 mm of mercury. Which statement is correct?","a":"atmospheric pressure supports the mercury column","unit":"","d1":"mercury pushes the air down","d2":"the tube must be sealed at both ends","d3":"the reading does not depend on air pressure","explain":"The atmosphere presses on the open mercury surface and supports the column in the tube.","formula":"P = ρgh","topic":"pressure","tier":3,"trap":"Reversing which side pushes, or assuming the tube is sealed."},{"q":"A straight wire 4 m long carries 3 A at right angles to a magnetic field of 2 T. What force acts on it?","a":"24","unit":"N","d1":"1.5","d2":"2.67","d3":"6","explain":"Force on a current-carrying wire is F = BIL = 2 × 3 × 4 = 24 N.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing by the length instead of multiplying."},{"q":"What is the unit of magnetic flux density?","a":"tesla","unit":"","d1":"weber","d2":"newton","d3":"ampere","explain":"Magnetic flux density B is measured in tesla (T).","formula":"","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing flux density with force or current."},{"q":"What happens when two north poles of magnets are brought together?","a":"they repel","unit":"","d1":"they attract","d2":"nothing happens","d3":"they cancel","explain":"Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.","formula":"","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Assuming all poles attract."},{"q":"What does a current-carrying wire placed in a magnetic field experience?","a":"a force","unit":"","d1":"no effect","d2":"an electric charge","d3":"a change of resistance","explain":"The motor effect: a current in a magnetic field feels a force perpendicular to both.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Thinking the field has no effect unless the wire moves."},{"q":"Which material is best for the core of an electromagnet?","a":"iron","unit":"","d1":"copper","d2":"plastic","d3":"wood","explain":"Iron is easily magnetised and unmagnetised, so it makes a strong electromagnet core.","formula":"","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Choosing copper because it conducts electricity well."},{"q":"A 3 m wire carries 2 A at right angles to a 0.5 T field. What force acts on it?","a":"3","unit":"N","d1":"0.333","d2":"0.75","d3":"12","explain":"F = BIL = 0.5 × 2 × 3 = 3 N.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Dividing by length instead of multiplying."},{"q":"A transformer has 200 turns on the primary and 100 turns on the secondary. The input is 12 V. What is the output?","a":"6","unit":"V","d1":"24","d2":"12","d3":"3","explain":"V2 = V1 × N2/N1 = 12 × 100/200 = 6 V.","formula":"V1/V2 = N1/N2","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Inverting the turns ratio, which gives 24 V."},{"q":"Outside a bar magnet, which way do magnetic field lines point?","a":"from north to south","unit":"","d1":"from south to north","d2":"in random directions","d3":"from both poles outwards","explain":"Field lines leave the north pole and enter the south pole outside the magnet.","formula":"","topic":"magnetism","tier":1,"trap":"Reversing the field direction, which is correct only inside the magnet."},{"q":"A wire experiences 12 N in a 3 T field over a length of 2 m. What current flows?","a":"2","unit":"A","d1":"72","d2":"4","d3":"8","explain":"Rearrange F = BIL to I = F/(BL) = 12/6 = 2 A.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying the force by field and length."},{"q":"A transformer has 400 primary turns and 100 secondary turns. The input is 240 V. What is the output?","a":"60","unit":"V","d1":"960","d2":"240","d3":"15","explain":"V2 = V1 × N2/N1 = 240 × 0.25 = 60 V.","formula":"V1/V2 = N1/N2","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the turns ratio, which gives 960 V."},{"q":"An ideal transformer takes 240 V at 0.5 A on the primary and outputs 12 V. What current flows in the secondary?","a":"10","unit":"A","d1":"0.5","d2":"20","d3":"0.025","explain":"Input power 120 W equals output power, so I2 = P/V2 = 120/12 = 10 A.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming the current stays the same, or using the voltage ratio the wrong way."},{"q":"A 10 N force acts on a 2 A current in a 5 m wire. What is the magnetic flux density?","a":"1","unit":"T","d1":"100","d2":"5","d3":"2","explain":"B = F/(IL) = 10/10 = 1 T.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Multiplying force by current and length."},{"q":"A transformer steps 100 V down to 25 V using 800 primary turns. How many secondary turns are needed?","a":"200","unit":"","d1":"3200","d2":"800","d3":"50","explain":"N2 = N1 × V2/V1 = 800 × 25/100 = 200 turns.","formula":"V1/V2 = N1/N2","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the voltage ratio, which gives 3200."},{"q":"An ideal transformer has 2 A in the primary and 0.5 A in the secondary at 100 V. What is the primary voltage?","a":"25","unit":"V","d1":"400","d2":"100","d3":"50","explain":"Power is equal: V1I1 = V2I2, so V1 = 100 × 0.5/2 = 25 V.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"magnetism","tier":2,"trap":"Inverting the current ratio, which gives 400 V."},{"q":"A wire 50 cm long carries 5 A at right angles to a 0.4 T field. What force acts on it?","a":"1","unit":"N","d1":"100","d2":"4","d3":"0.04","explain":"Convert 50 cm to 0.5 m, then F = 0.4 × 5 × 0.5 = 1 N.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Using centimetres directly, which gives 100 N."},{"q":"A transformer takes 240 V at 0.5 A and delivers 12 V at 8 A. What is its efficiency?","a":"80","unit":"%","d1":"96","d2":"8","d3":"12","explain":"Input power is 120 W and output 96 W, so efficiency = 96/120 × 100 = 80%.","formula":"efficiency = output/input × 100","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the output power as a percentage, or the difference in powers."},{"q":"An ideal transformer has 240 V and 2 A on the primary and 24 V on the secondary. What is the secondary current?","a":"20","unit":"A","d1":"2","d2":"0.2","d3":"240","explain":"Power is conserved: 480 W output at 24 V gives 20 A.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Scaling the current by the voltage ratio the wrong way."},{"q":"A 2 m wire carries 3 A at right angles to a 0.2 T field. What force acts on it?","a":"1.2","unit":"N","d1":"0.3","d2":"0.133","d3":"30","explain":"F = BIL = 0.2 × 3 × 2 = 1.2 N.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing by length instead of multiplying."},{"q":"A wire 25 cm long carries 4 A at right angles to a 0.5 T field. What force acts on it?","a":"0.5","unit":"N","d1":"50","d2":"8","d3":"0.0312","explain":"Convert 25 cm to 0.25 m, then F = 0.5 × 4 × 0.25 = 0.5 N.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Using centimetres directly, which gives 50 N."},{"q":"A transformer steps 240 V down to 6 V using 1200 primary turns. How many secondary turns?","a":"30","unit":"","d1":"48000","d2":"600","d3":"60","explain":"N2 = N1 × V2/V1 = 1200 × 6/240 = 30 turns.","formula":"V1/V2 = N1/N2","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Inverting the voltage ratio, which gives 48000."},{"q":"A transformer has 100 W input and 90 W output. What is its efficiency?","a":"90","unit":"%","d1":"10","d2":"110","d3":"9","explain":"Efficiency = useful output/input × 100 = 90/100 × 100 = 90%.","formula":"efficiency = output/input × 100","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the loss as the efficiency."},{"q":"An ideal transformer has 3 A at 20 V on the secondary and 240 V on the primary. What is the primary current?","a":"0.25","unit":"A","d1":"3","d2":"12","d3":"36","explain":"Power is 60 W, so I1 = P/V1 = 60/240 = 0.25 A.","formula":"P = IV","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Scaling the current up with the voltage ratio."},{"q":"A 6 N force acts on a 4 A current in a 0.5 m wire. What is the magnetic flux density?","a":"3","unit":"T","d1":"12","d2":"1.5","d3":"48","explain":"B = F/(IL) = 6/2 = 3 T.","formula":"F = BIL","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Multiplying force by current and length."},{"q":"Two parallel wires carry current in the same direction. What force acts between them?","a":"they attract","unit":"","d1":"they repel","d2":"no force","d3":"they rotate","explain":"Parallel currents in the same direction produce magnetic fields that attract the wires.","formula":"","topic":"magnetism","tier":3,"trap":"Assuming same-direction currents repel like poles."},{"q":"What is the weight of a 50 kg astronaut on the Moon? (g = 1.6 m/s²)","a":"80","unit":"N","d1":"490","d2":"50","d3":"31.25","explain":"Weight is W = mg, and the Moon's g is 1.6 m/s², so W = 80 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Using Earth's g, or reporting the mass."},{"q":"Which is the correct order of the first four planets from the Sun?","a":"Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars","unit":"","d1":"Venus, Mercury, Earth, Mars","d2":"Mercury, Earth, Venus, Mars","d3":"Mars, Earth, Venus, Mercury","explain":"The inner planets in order are Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Swapping Mercury and Venus, or reversing the order."},{"q":"What causes day and night on Earth?","a":"Earth rotating on its axis","unit":"","d1":"Earth orbiting the Sun","d2":"the Moon orbiting Earth","d3":"Earth's axis tilting","explain":"Earth turns once on its axis every 24 hours, so each side faces the Sun in turn.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing rotation with the yearly orbit."},{"q":"How long does Earth take to complete one orbit of the Sun?","a":"365 days","unit":"","d1":"24 hours","d2":"28 days","d3":"12 hours","explain":"One orbit takes about 365 days, which defines the year. This is a remembered value, not a calculation.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing the day with the year."},{"q":"A light year is a unit of what?","a":"distance","unit":"","d1":"time","d2":"speed","d3":"brightness","explain":"A light year is the distance light travels in one year.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Assuming it measures time because of the word year."},{"q":"What is the name of the galaxy containing the Sun?","a":"the Milky Way","unit":"","d1":"Andromeda","d2":"the Solar System","d3":"Orion","explain":"The Sun lies in the Milky Way galaxy; the Solar System is a much smaller object within it.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing the galaxy with the Solar System or a constellation."},{"q":"What force keeps the planets moving in orbit around the Sun?","a":"gravity","unit":"","d1":"magnetism","d2":"friction","d3":"tension","explain":"The Sun's gravitational attraction provides the centripetal force for each orbit.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Choosing another force that does not act across space."},{"q":"Earth's axis is tilted. What does this tilt cause?","a":"the seasons","unit":"","d1":"day and night","d2":"the tides","d3":"the phases of the Moon","explain":"The tilt changes the angle of sunlight through the year, producing the seasons.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":1,"trap":"Confusing the tilt with the rotation that gives day and night."},{"q":"What is the weight of a 20 kg rover on Mars? (g = 3.7 m/s²)","a":"74","unit":"N","d1":"196","d2":"20","d3":"5.41","explain":"W = mg = 20 × 3.7 = 74 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Using Earth's g, or reporting the mass."},{"q":"Light travels at 3 × 10^8 m/s. How far does it travel in one year? (1 year = 3.15 × 10^7 s)","a":"9450000000000000","unit":"m","d1":"9.52","d2":"9450000000000","d3":"992250000000000","explain":"Distance = speed × time = (3 × 10^8) × (3.15 × 10^7) = 9.45 × 10^15 m.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing speed by time, or losing a power of ten."},{"q":"A planet moves at 30000 m/s and takes 100000 s to complete an orbit. How far does it travel?","a":"3000000000","unit":"m","d1":"0.3","d2":"3000000","d3":"130000","explain":"Distance = speed × time = 30000 × 100000 = 3 × 10^9 m.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying."},{"q":"What causes the phases of the Moon?","a":"the changing angle between the Sun, Earth and Moon","unit":"","d1":"Earth's shadow falling on the Moon","d2":"clouds covering the Moon","d3":"the Moon spinning on its axis","explain":"We see different fractions of the Moon's lit half as it orbits Earth.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Confusing phases with a lunar eclipse, which is Earth's shadow."},{"q":"What is the weight of a 10 kg object on Jupiter? (g = 24.8 m/s²)","a":"248","unit":"N","d1":"98","d2":"10","d3":"0.403","explain":"W = mg = 10 × 24.8 = 248 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Using Earth's g, or dividing by g."},{"q":"The gravitational force between two objects decreases when what happens?","a":"the distance between them increases","unit":"","d1":"their masses increase","d2":"the distance between them decreases","d3":"their temperature increases","explain":"Gravity weakens with distance, following an inverse-square law.","formula":"F = Gm1m2/r²","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Reversing the distance dependence."},{"q":"Compared with Earth, a year on a planet further from the Sun is","a":"longer","unit":"","d1":"shorter","d2":"the same","d3":"zero","explain":"A larger orbit means a longer path and a slower average speed, so the year is longer.","formula":"","topic":"space","tier":2,"trap":"Assuming the year is the same everywhere."},{"q":"A satellite orbits at 7.5 km/s and takes 90 minutes. How far does it travel in one orbit? Give your answer in km.","a":"40500","unit":"km","d1":"675","d2":"40.5","d3":"2430000","explain":"Time is 90 × 60 = 5400 s, so distance = 7.5 × 5400 = 40500 km.","formula":"d = vt","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Using minutes instead of seconds, or converting km to m by mistake."},{"q":"The Sun is 1.5 × 10^11 m from Earth. How long does sunlight take to reach us? (c = 3 × 10^8 m/s)","a":"500","unit":"s","d1":"8.33","d2":"500000","d3":"0.002","explain":"t = d/c = (1.5 × 10^11)/(3 × 10^8) = 500 s.","formula":"t = d/c","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing the other way, or converting seconds to minutes."},{"q":"One light year is 9.46 × 10^15 m. How many metres are in 4.2 light years?","a":"39732000000000000","unit":"m","d1":"0","d2":"2250000000000000","d3":"39732000000000","explain":"Multiply: 4.2 × 9.46 × 10^15 = 3.97 × 10^16 m.","formula":"distance = number of light years × 9.46 × 10^15","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying, or losing three powers of ten."},{"q":"A planet orbits at radius 2.0 × 10^11 m with a period of 6.0 × 10^7 s. What is its orbital speed? (take π = 3.14)","a":"20900","unit":"m/s","d1":"3330","d2":"1256000","d3":"0.0019","explain":"Speed = circumference/period = (2 × 3.14 × 2.0 × 10^11)/(6.0 × 10^7) = 2.09 × 10^4 m/s.","formula":"v = 2πr/T","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Using the radius as the distance travelled, or omitting 2π."},{"q":"A 70 kg astronaut weighs 686 N on Earth. What is the difference between her weight on Earth and on Mars? (g_Mars = 3.7 m/s²)","a":"427","unit":"N","d1":"259","d2":"686","d3":"945","explain":"Mars weight is 259 N, so the difference is 686 - 259 = 427 N.","formula":"W = mg","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Reporting the Mars weight, or adding the two weights."},{"q":"A star is 4.0 × 10^16 m away. How many years does its light take to reach Earth? (1 ly = 9.46 × 10^15 m)","a":"4.23","unit":"years","d1":"42.3","d2":"0.236","d3":"0.0423","explain":"Convert to light years: (4.0 × 10^16)/(9.46 × 10^15) = 4.23 years.","formula":"time = distance/light year","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Multiplying by the light-year value instead of dividing."},{"q":"Earth orbits at radius 1.5 × 10^11 m. What is its orbital speed? (1 year = 3.15 × 10^7 s, π = 3.14)","a":"29900","unit":"m/s","d1":"4760","d2":"942000000000","d3":"0","explain":"Speed = 2πr/T = (2 × 3.14 × 1.5 × 10^11)/(3.15 × 10^7) = 2.99 × 10^4 m/s.","formula":"v = 2πr/T","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Using r/T, which omits the circumference factor 2π."},{"q":"A moon orbits a planet at 1.0 × 10^4 m/s with an orbital radius of 2.0 × 10^8 m. What is its period? (π = 3.14)","a":"125600","unit":"s","d1":"20000","d2":"0.0003","d3":"12560000000000","explain":"T = circumference/speed = (2 × 3.14 × 2.0 × 10^8)/(1.0 × 10^4) = 1.26 × 10^5 s.","formula":"T = 2πr/v","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing radius by speed, or inverting the formula."},{"q":"A comet has an orbital period of 76 years. How many seconds is this? (1 year = 3.15 × 10^7 s)","a":"2394000000","unit":"s","d1":"0","d2":"2394000","d3":"414000","explain":"Multiply: 76 × 3.15 × 10^7 = 2.39 × 10^9 s.","formula":"time = years × 3.15 × 10^7","topic":"space","tier":3,"trap":"Dividing instead of multiplying, or losing powers of ten."}]
